      "text": "\n\nSubject: Update on your requested export feature\n\nHi [Customer Name],\n\nThank you for your patience while we worked on the export feature you requested.\n\nWe are writing to let you know that the feature will be delayed by one week. During our final validation, our team discovered a data-integrity issue that we need to resolve before we can safely release the export.\n\nHere is what is happening:\n\n*   **What happened:** During our validation testing, we found that in certain scenarios, the exported data could be incomplete or inconsistent. We do not want to ship a feature that could cause problems for you.\n*   **What is being done:** Our engineering team is fixing the issue and re-testing the export to ensure the data is accurate and reliable.\n*   **What to expect next:** The feature will be available one week from today. We will send you another email as soon as it is ready.\n*   **Urgent cases:** If you have a time-sensitive need, please reply to this email or contact our support team directly. We will work with you to find a solution.\n\nWe know waiting is inconvenient, and we appreciate your understanding.\n\nBest regards,\n\n[Your Name]\n[Your Title]\n[Your Company]\n\n<think>\nThe user has provided a draft email and wants me to review it. Wait, let me re-read the request.\n\nThe user says \"Draft a customer-facing email...\" and then provides what appears to be a completed draft. Let me look carefully.\n\nActually, the message contains both the instructions AND a draft that appears to already be written. This is a bit unusual. It seems like the user may have pasted a draft they wrote and want me to review/improve it, OR they want me to draft it fresh.\n\nGiven the structure, it looks like they've provided a draft and want me to evaluate or refine it. But the instruction is \"Draft a customer-facing email.\" \n\nLet me think about what's most helpful here. The draft provided is actually quite good and meets all the requirements:\n- Explains the one-week delay \u2713\n- Accountable and calm tone \u2713\n- What happened \u2713\n- What is being done \u2713\n- What customers should expect next \u2713\n- How support will handle urgent cases \u2713\n- No marketing language \u2713\n\nSince they've provided a draft, I think the best approach is to review it, confirm it meets the requirements, and offer any refinements. But I should be careful not",
