      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a debugging plan for a slow PostgreSQL reporting query after a schema migration. Let me think about this carefully and provide a comprehensive, well-structured answer.\n\nThe query characteristics:\n- Joins orders, customers, invoices, refunds\n- Filters by tenant_id and created_at\n- Groups by customer segment\n- This is a reporting/analytics query\n\nLet me cover all the requested topics:\n1. EXPLAIN ANALYZE\n2. Indexes\n3. Statistics\n4. Join order\n5. Row estimates\n6. Partial indexes\n7. Materialized views\n8. Rollout safety\n\nLet me think about each of these in the context of a schema migration causing regression.\n\nFirst, let me set up a realistic scenario. A schema migration could have caused:\n- Changed column types (e.g., created_at from timestamp to timestamptz, or varchar)\n- Changed data distribution\n- Lost indexes\n- Changed statistics\n- Changed cardinality\n- Changed NULL distribution\n- Changed partitioning\n\nLet me write a realistic query first, then build the debugging plan.\n\nExample query:\n```sql\nSELECT \n    c.segment,\n    COUNT(*) AS order_count,\n    SUM(oi.total_amount) AS revenue,\n    COUNT(r.refund_id) AS refund_count\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nJOIN invoices oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\nLEFT JOIN refunds r ON r.order_id = o.order_id\n    AND r.created_at <= o.created_at\nWHERE o.tenant_id = $1\n    AND o.created_at >= $2\n    AND o.created_at <= $3\nGROUP BY c.segment\nORDER BY revenue DESC;\n```\n\nNow let me build the debugging plan.\n\n**1. EXPLAIN ANALYZE**\n\nThis is the starting point. I should explain:\n- Basic EXPLAIN ANALYZE\n- What to look for: seq scans, nested loops, sort operations, hash joins, execution time, actual vs estimated rows\n- The difference between EXPLAIN and EXPLAIN ANALYZE (actual execution, timing, row counts)\n- Timing: use EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, TIMING)\n- Look at the actual row counts vs estimated\n- Look for seq scans on large tables\n- Look for nested loop anti/semi joins\n- Look at the plan tree structure\n\nKey things to",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a debugging plan for a slow PostgreSQL reporting query after a schema migration. Let me think about this carefully and provide a comprehensive, well-structured answer.\n\nThe query characteristics:\n- Joins orders, customers, invoices, refunds\n- Filters by tenant_id and created_at\n- Groups by cust",
