      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user is asking me to analyze a benchmark result scenario where a model server shows high tokens per second on repeated prompts but much lower speed on unique prompts. This is a classic benchmarking artifact issue. Let me think through this carefully.\n\nThis is a technical question about benchmarking LLM model servers (like vLLM, TGI, SGLang, etc.). The scenario describes a common problem: when you run the same prompt multiple times, you get high throughput, but when you run unique/fresh prompts, throughput is much lower.\n\nLet me break this down into the components they're asking for:\n\n1. **Explain plausible causes** - Why would repeated prompts be faster than unique prompts?\n2. **How to separate valid fresh-response throughput from warmed/cached throughput** - Methodology\n3. **What measurements should be recorded before publishing a result** - Best practices\n\nLet me think about the causes first.\n\n**Causes of the discrepancy:**\n\n1. **KV Cache reuse / prompt caching**: When the same prompt is repeated, the key-value cache can be reused. Many serving frameworks (vLLM, SGLang) support prompt caching where if a prompt prefix matches what's already in the cache, the computation for that prefix is skipped. This dramatically speeds up repeated prompts.\n\n2. **Model warmup**: The first requests trigger various initialization steps:\n   - Loading the model weights into VRAM\n   - Compiling kernels (especially with Triton, CUDA graphs)\n   - JIT compilation of various operators\n   - Warmup of the CUDA graph capture\n   - Memory allocation patterns settling\n   - The first few requests may trigger page faults, memory allocation, etc.\n\n3. **CUDA Graph capture**: Some servers capture CUDA graphs after warmup. The first requests are slow because they're not part of the graph; subsequent requests replay the graph which is much faster.\n\n4. **Memory allocation and fragmentation**: First requests may trigger memory allocation, and the allocator may need to settle into an efficient state.\n\n5. **Prefill vs decode ratio**: If the benchmark uses prompts of varying lengths, and repeated prompts happen to be shorter or have already been prefilled...\n\n6. **Batching effects**: If repeated prompts can be batched together efficiently, but unique prompts arrive at different times...\n\n7. **Prompt caching specifically**: This is the big one. If you run the same prompt 100 times, the framework caches the KV",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\nThe user is asking me to analyze a benchmark result scenario where a model server shows high tokens per second on repeated prompts but much lower speed on unique prompts. This is a classic benchmarking artifact issue. Let me think through this carefully.\n\nThis is a technical question about benchmarking LLM mo",
