      "request_id": "bench-qwen36-27b-autoround-int4-b70-realistic-v1-09-risk-register",
      "request_started_epoch_s": 1787874015.9297764,
      "response_id_first": null,
      "response_id_last": null,
      "response_x_request_id": null,
      "sha256": "906e4866dfa8538f2a04bd8d41ad4d1a559caf7c29d8a960fec6b3d4615901e9",
      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a risk register for switching a production inference endpoint from one runtime to another. This is a very specific, technical operational task. Let me think about what actually goes wrong when you switch inference runtimes in production.\n\nFirst, let me understand the context. An \"inference endpoint\" is an API that runs machine learning models to make predictions. \"Switching from one runtime to another\" could mean:\n- Switching from one ML framework runtime (e.g., PyTorch to TensorFlow, or ONNX Runtime to Triton)\n- Switching container runtimes (Docker to something else)\n- Switching serving frameworks (TorchServe to Triton Inference Server, or KServe, etc.)\n- Switching from a managed service to self-hosted\n- Switching from one language runtime (Python to Go, etc.)\n\nThe most common and realistic scenario is switching inference serving runtimes/frameworks. Let me focus on the operational and quality risks that are real and specific to this kind of change.\n\nLet me brainstorm real risks:\n\n1. **Numerical precision / model output drift** - Different runtimes may produce slightly different outputs due to different numerical libraries, kernels, or default settings. This can cause quality degradation that's subtle. Severity: High. Likelihood: Medium. Detection: A/B comparison, shadow traffic, output distribution monitoring. Mitigation: Run both in parallel, compare distributions, use tolerance thresholds. Rollback trigger: Output distribution shift beyond threshold, or accuracy drop on validation set.\n\n2. **Model format / serialization incompatibility** - The model may be saved in a format the new runtime doesn't support, or requires conversion. Severity: High. Likelihood: Medium-High. Detection: Load test fails, conversion errors. Mitigation: Validate model compatibility before cutover, convert models early, test with representative models. Rollback trigger: Model fails to load.\n\n3. **Latency / throughput regression** - New runtime may be slower or have different performance characteristics. Severity: High. Likelihood: Medium. Detection: Latency p99 monitoring, throughput metrics. Mitigation: Load testing with representative traffic, capacity planning, warmup. Rollback trigger: Latency exceeds SLO, throughput drops below threshold.\n\n4. **Memory / resource exhaustion** - New runtime may use more memory, or have memory leaks, or not handle batch sizes well. Severity: High. Likelihood: Medium. Detection: Memory utilization",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a risk register for switching a production inference endpoint from one runtime to another. This is a very specific, technical operational task. Let me think about what actually goes wrong when you switch inference runtimes in production.\n\nFirst, let me understand the context. An \"inference endp",
